From 1908e7a62f3a9c962cf51d7b87746a36e626f42f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 17:25:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm, VideoRenderers: leave the GUI pass out while a
 subtitle stays

Where the GUI is composited over the picture (HDR10, HLG, Dolby Vision
rebuilt as HDR10, native Dolby Vision output), the GUI pass of a frame is
left out where no control is dirty (CDVGuiReuse), but it was not while
subtitles or the debug information were on screen: they were "not left to
the dirty regions alone". With a text subtitle on screen the pass was drawn
in every frame: the FBO cleared, the line drawn again and the FBO
composited over the whole window, for as long as the line stayed. With the
PGS of an HDR video, which is drawn with the picture and not in the GUI
pass, the walk of every control was kept for nothing.

Subtitles do mark the GUI dirty where they change: libass tells a change
(detect_change) and a bitmap that arrives has no texture yet
(CRenderer::PrepareOverlays()), and a mark made after the windows were
processed takes the skip back in that frame (CApplication::FrameMove(),
MarkedSinceProcess()). What nothing told was a subtitle that goes without
another coming: one of two bitmaps that ends, text subtitles that are turned
off. PrepareOverlays() now keeps the set of the subtitles on screen
(OverlayVisibleSet.h: the bitmap overlays, and whether libass gave images)
and marks the GUI dirty where it is not the set of the frame before, which
also covers the last bitmap that goes (the flag that did that is gone).

The fact that keeps the pass in every frame is then the debug information
alone, which changes without marking anything (SetGuiPassDebug()). A
subtitle is drawn in the frame it comes with, and while it stays the pass is
left out: the FBO of the frame before is what the pass would draw, and it is
composited under the tile mask of the GUI once that is back, over the band
of the subtitle instead of the whole window. An animated line (karaoke, a
move) changes in every frame and has the pass in every frame, as before.
Native Dolby Vision output takes the same rule: its frames with a subtitle
stay composed (m_dvOverlays), the packer composites the same GUI texture,
and its mask is the one of the content of the GUI that was last drawn.

Tests (outside the tree): pgs_reuse_test, a model of the frame with the real
rule and the real set (a still line; one that comes, changes and goes;
subtitles turned off; one of two bitmaps that ends; an animated line; the
OSD over a line; the debug information, and turning it off; the PGS of an
HDR video), where every frame holds what a pass in every frame would draw,
the old rule fails on the passes it draws, and mutants without the set,
without the late mark or without the debug information fail on the frames;
pgs_reuse_wiring.py, the real code (fails on the tree before); text_frame,
the frames on llvmpipe and zink, full and limited range, the same bytes as
a pass in every frame in each case; the other host tests of the GUI reuse and of the frame pass.
